  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nLight rain showers continue to traverse across north central and \ninto northeastern South Dakota this afternoon. A layer of dry air in \nthe lower levels means that rain has struggles to reach the surface \nat times, but the strongest showers have dropped a trace to a couple \nhundredths of rain in their wake. Expectations for the next couple \nof hours is that these virga to occasional light showers will \ncontinue to struggle to get rain to the surface as they push north \nand east this afternoon. \n\nOvernight tonight, there are some signals for another round of fog \ndevelopment. Confidence is highest over parts of north central South \nDakota where full humidity recovery is expected for an extended \nperiod tonight. Weak easterly flow will also help due to gradual \nterrain increases providing a bit of an upslope effect in that area. \nCoverage of fog is expected to be fairly patchy, but there is some \nsignal for visibility to drop below a mile at times. The other \npotential area of concern would be localized areas on the eastern \nedge of the Prairie Coteau, where upslope impacts once again assist \nin fog formation. Confidence is perhaps a bit lower in that area, \nbut visibility may still drop to a mile at times. \n\nThe next round of precipitation will move into the area Friday into \nSaturday. Showers will move up into central South Dakota during the \nlate afternoon to evening hours before spreading to the entire \nforecast area overnight and exiting to the east Saturday morning. In \ngeneral rain rates with these showers will be light, and most areas \nwill see just a few hundredths. Latest probabilities to see a tenth \nof an inch range from 10-40 percent, increasing as you move from \nnorth to south across the forecast area. Can't entirely rule out a \nweak thunderstorm out of this system, but a mid-level inversion \naround 750mb will likely limit the growth of these showers. \n\nA Colorado Low will track into the region early next week, bringing \nanother round of precipitation into the area. Overnight lows in the \nupper 30s to 40s support this precipitation being all rain with this \nsystem. Current expectations are that precipitation will move in \nMonday morning over central South Dakota, spreading across the rest \nof the forecast area the rest of Monday and into Tuesday. The bulk \nof the rain is expected through Tuesday morning, and the chances to \nsee an inch of rain or more through that time period ranges from 30 \nto 60 percent, increasing moving from northwest to southeast across \nthe forecast area. There is still a high degree of spread still \nbased on uncertainty in the timing and track of the low. Notably, \nthe Euro ensemble tracks the low pressure center just a bit further \nnorth than other ensembles do, which would result in the Aberdeen \nforecast area trending towards higher rainfall totals through the \nevent.",
